# Unity brain control plane — narrow privilege for brain-ctl.js.
#
# Grants the `unity` service account exactly ONE command with no password:
# the fixed-path helper at /usr/local/sbin/brain-ctl-helper, which itself only
# accepts a closed set of verbs against the single unit "unity-brain"
# (see deploy/brain-ctl-helper.sh).
#
# WHY NOT `NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start unity-brain` DIRECTLY:
# This box also runs Forgejo and the lab's git. A sudoers entry naming systemctl
# grants a general-purpose privileged tool, and sudoers argument matching is
# easy to get subtly wrong (wildcards, argument reordering, and several
# systemctl verbs that can be steered at other units). Naming ONE purpose-built
# script keeps the privileged surface auditable in a single file, and that file
# hardcodes the unit name rather than accepting it from the caller.
#
# NOTE the helper takes NO arguments from the network. brain-ctl.js only ever
# passes hardcoded argv from its own allowlist, and the helper re-validates.
#
# The trailing "" means: no arguments other than those matched. We deliberately
# allow any args here because the HELPER is the validator (it rejects unknown
# verbs with exit 77/64); pinning argument patterns in sudoers as well would
# duplicate that logic in the one place it is hardest to test.
#
# Install:
#   sudo install -o root -g root -m 440 deploy/sudoers.d/unity-brain-ctl /etc/sudoers.d/unity-brain-ctl
#   sudo visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/unity-brain-ctl    # MUST print "parsed OK"
#
# A malformed sudoers file can lock everyone out of sudo — ALWAYS visudo -c it.

unity ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/brain-ctl-helper
